.TH KEYRING-GETSTRING 2
.SH NAME
keyring: getstring, putstring, getbytearray, putbytearray, puterror \- exchange data on delimited streams
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "keyring.m"
keyring:= load Keyring Keyring->PATH;

getstring:    fn(fd: ref Sys->FD): (string, string);
putstring:    fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, s: string): int;
getbytearray: fn(fd: ref Sys->FD): (array of byte, string);
putbytearray: fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, a: array of byte, n: int): int;
puterror:     fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, s: string): int;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions provide
I/O for strings, byte arrays and error strings over network connections that
provide a record structure for communication (as provided for arbitrary
networks by
.IR ssl (3)).
.PP
.B Putstring
writes string
.I s
to
.IR fd.
It returns the number of bytes written, or -1 if an error occurred.
.PP
.B Getstring
reads a string as written by
.B putstring
from
.IR fd
and returns a tuple
.RI ( result , error ).
If successful, the error
string is nil.
.PP
.B Putbytearray
writes the array of bytes
.I a
to
.IR fd .
It returns the number of bytes written, or -1 if an error occurred.
.PP
.B Getbytearray
reads an array of bytes as written by
.B putbytearray
from
.IR fd
and returns a tuple of the form
.RI ( result , error ).
If successful, the error string is nil.
.PP
.B Puterror
writes an error string
.I s
to
.IR fd .
It can be used in place of
.B putstring
or
.B putbytearray
to cause a corresponding
.B getstring
or
.B getbytearray
to fail
(in the receiving process),
forcing them to return the error string
.IR s ,
whole.
It may not be longer than 4095 bytes.
.PP
A message of up to 4095 bytes is one write: a byte of zero
(of 255 for an error) and the data, and it is read by one read.
A longer one, of up to
.B Keyring->Maxmsg
(49151) bytes, is a byte of 1, its length as four bytes, most significant first,
and the data, and is read until that many bytes have arrived:
.IR ssl (3)
sends a write of more than 32K as several records.
The limit holds the text of the largest certificate
.RI ( keyring-certtostr (2))
of the signature algorithms offered, with room.
A message longer than that is not sent
.RB ( -1 ,
and the system error string is
.RB `` "message too long" '');
a receiver given a length beyond it returns that error and allocates nothing.
Nothing is cut.
(Messages used to be cut to 4095 bytes without a word, and an error's text to 63:
a certificate of ML-DSA-87, 6197 bytes of text, arrived as its first two thirds.
A receiver from before the longer form answers one with the error
.RB `` failure ''.)
.SH SOURCE
.B /libinterp/keyring.c
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The output functions return an
.B int
which is -1 if there was an I/O error,
and a non-negative value otherwise.
The input functions
return a tuple that includes a string indicating the cause of the
error, as the second element of the tuple.
